\description{
Diversity grows with sequencing depth, so samples of unequal depth can't
be compared directly. \code{hill_extrapolation()} gives each sample's Hill
numbers of order \eqn{q = 0, 1, 2} (richness, Shannon diversity and
Simpson diversity) at a range of depths, by \strong{rarefaction}
(interpolation) below the sample's size and \strong{extrapolation} above it,
with the \strong{sample coverage} at each depth and the \strong{asymptotic}
estimates (Chao et al. 2014), as \code{iNEXT::iNEXT()} does.
\code{hill_standardize()} gives the Hill numbers of every sample at one
common depth or, by default, one common coverage (as
\code{iNEXT::estimateD()}): values that can be compared, and tested with
\code{alpha_test()}.
}
\section{Rarefaction, extrapolation and coverage}{

For a sample of \eqn{n} observations with \eqn{f_k} features seen
\eqn{k} times:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Rarefaction} to \eqn{m < n}: the expected Hill number of a random
subsample of \eqn{m} of the observations (Chao et al. 2014, Table 1):
richness \eqn{S_{obs} - \sum_i \binom{n - X_i}{m} / \binom{n}{m}} (as
\code{\link[=rarefaction_curve]{rarefaction_curve()}}), Shannon diversity from the expected frequency
counts of the subsample, and Simpson diversity \eqn{1 / [1/m + (m-1)/m
  \sum_i X_i (X_i - 1) / (n (n - 1))]}.
\item \strong{Extrapolation} to \eqn{n + m^*}: richness \eqn{S_{obs} + \hat f_0
  [1 - (1 - f_1 / (n \hat f_0 + f_1))^{m^*}]}, with Chao1's \eqn{\hat
  f_0}; Shannon diversity \eqn{D_{obs} + (\hat D - D_{obs}) [1 - (1 -
  \hat\beta)^{m^*}]} (Chao et al. 2023, Table 2), from the observed and
asymptotic \eqn{\hat D}, with \eqn{\hat\beta = (D_{obs} - D(n - 1))
  / (\hat D - D(n - 1))}, so that the curve passes through the rarefied
value at \eqn{n - 1} (for richness, this \eqn{\hat\beta} is
\eqn{f_1 / (n \hat f_0 + f_1)}); Simpson diversity as for rarefaction,
with \eqn{n + m^*} for \eqn{m}.
\item \strong{Sample coverage}, the share of the community's individuals that
belong to features seen in a sample of that depth (Chao & Jost 2012),
by its rarefied and extrapolated estimates, with Chao et al.'s (2014,
Eq. 12) estimate at \eqn{n}.
\item \strong{Asymptotes}, the estimates for a complete sample: Chao1 (bias
corrected), \eqn{\exp(\hat H)}, and \eqn{n (n - 1) / \sum_i X_i (X_i -
  1)}.
}

Between two whole depths (when a coverage level falls between them),
every value is the straight line between its values at those depths, as
in iNEXT.

\strong{Reliability:} richness can be extrapolated reliably to about twice the
sample's size; Shannon and Simpson diversity, if the data aren't too
sparse, to their asymptotes (Chao et al. 2014). \code{max_depth} beyond twice
a sample's size is allowed, but read richness there with care.
}

\section{Equal depth or equal coverage}{

Comparing samples at an equal \strong{depth} says how diverse equally large
samples would be; comparing them at an equal \strong{coverage} compares
equally \emph{complete} samples, which Chao & Jost (2012) recommend: a more
diverse community needs more observations to reach the same coverage,
and comparing at equal depth understates its difference from a less
diverse one. \code{hill_standardize()} uses coverage unless you give
\code{depth}; its default coverage, the smallest among the samples
extrapolated to twice their size, is as far as every sample can be
extrapolated reliably.
}

\section{Confidence intervals}{

With \code{n_boot > 0}, each estimate gets a bootstrap standard error and the
interval estimate \eqn{\pm z_{(1 + \text{conf\_level})/2}} SE (Chao et
al. 2014, Appendix G): \code{n_boot} times, a sample of the same size is drawn
from the estimated community (the observed features, with their
relative abundances adjusted for coverage, plus Chao1's undetected
ones), and every estimate is recomputed from it. At a fixed coverage,
each bootstrap sample's own depth for that coverage is used. Lower
limits stop at 0 (and the asymptotic richness's at the observed
richness), and coverage limits at 0 and 1, as in iNEXT.

Replicate \eqn{b} of sample \eqn{i} has its own random stream, from
\code{seed}, so the results are the same for any \code{cpus}. The cost grows with
\code{n_boot + 1} and with the number of depths (\code{n_depths}): set \code{n_boot = 0}
for estimates alone.
}

\section{Differences from iNEXT}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{The estimates} equal iNEXT 3.0.2's to rounding, including Shannon
diversity beyond the sample's size, which iNEXT 3 (and ecodive) take
from Chao et al. (2023) rather than Chao et al.'s (2014) Eq. 10c.
\item \strong{The bootstrap} draws from ecodive's own random streams, so standard
errors and intervals agree with iNEXT's only within Monte Carlo error.
The asymptotes' intervals use the same \code{n_boot} replicates as the
curves; iNEXT's always use 100 (whatever \code{nboot}).
\item \strong{Coverage-based depths} are found exactly; iNEXT finds them by a
numerical search, which leaves its depths (and its estimates there)
off by about \eqn{10^{-5}}. Where coverage is flat over a range of
depths, ecodive takes the smallest, and gives one row; iNEXT's search
lands anywhere in the range, and repeats the row.
\item With \code{depths}, each sample's own size is added (iNEXT also adds
\eqn{n - 1} and \eqn{n + 1}). With \code{n_depths} below 4, iNEXT's grid
differs.
\item Abundance data only, \eqn{q \in \{0, 1, 2\}}, and the results' shapes
(below). \code{hill_standardize()} holds coverage equal by default;
\code{estimateD()} holds depth equal (\code{base = "size"}).
}
}

\section{Same names in other packages}{

None. The same estimates: \code{iNEXT::iNEXT()}, \code{iNEXT::estimateD()},
\code{iNEXT::DataInfo()}, \code{iNEXT::ChaoRichness()}, \code{iNEXT::ChaoShannon()} and
\code{iNEXT::ChaoSimpson()}.
}

\section{The depths}{

With \code{depths = NULL}, each sample of size \eqn{n} with endpoint \eqn{M}
(\code{max_depth}, else \eqn{2n}) gets iNEXT's grid of \eqn{K} = \code{n_depths}
knots: if \eqn{M > n}, \eqn{\lfloor K/2 \rfloor - 1} depths evenly from 1
to \eqn{n - 1}, then \eqn{n}, then \eqn{\lfloor K/2 \rfloor} from \eqn{n +
1} to \eqn{M}; else \eqn{K} from 1 to \eqn{M}. Each is rounded down, and
duplicates dropped.
}

\examples{
    # Richness, Shannon and Simpson diversity of each sample, by depth.
    he <- hill_extrapolation(ex_counts, n_boot = 10)
    he
    head(he$asymptotic)
    plot(he, q = 1)
    plot(he, type = 'coverage')

    # Every sample at an equal coverage, then at an equal depth.
    hill_standardize(ex_counts, n_boot = 10)
    hill_standardize(ex_counts, depth = 1000, n_boot = 0)

}
